issue(id="0007" status="open")
  title Land baseline HTML-to-Pug correction and consistency fixes
  user-story
    as-a maintainer of the puggers HTML converter
    i-want the importer to stop making a few avoidable normalization mistakes and to align more closely with the formatter's current whitespace and text-handling model
    so-that users can shape bulky HTML into the concise Pug view that best surfaces the story they care about
  premise.
    The HTML converter is not just an "import" step in the narrow sense. Its
    real job is to turn bulky markup into a more concise Pug representation that
    foregrounds signal and suppresses noise. That means its defaults can stay
    opinionated, but those opinions must be intentional and adjustable because
    "signal" depends on the user's goal: turning a webpage into a readable
    document view, inspecting utility-class structure, studying comments,
    preserving text boundaries for later editing, or extracting some other
    structural story from the sample.
    The current converter already makes several strong choices: it removes all
    non-allowlisted attributes, collapses normal text whitespace aggressively,
    trims comments and inline text, recognizes only a short raw-text tag list,
    and offers only coarse outer-document and wrapper-collapse controls. Those
    defaults are useful for a first pass, but several were stronger than the
    formatter's own whitespace-sensitive model in avoidable ways. This task is
    now centered on baseline corrections: preserving meaningful inline spacing
    when requested, preserving comments more faithfully by default, and making
    line-width-driven import output behave more like the formatter's current
    prose and wrapping model without inventing HTML semantics that do not exist.
    Broader questions such as full formatter-option parity, importer presets,
    richer region-selection controls, and code sharing with the formatter should
    be treated as follow-on tasks instead of keeping `0007` indefinitely broad.
  acceptance-criteria
    criterion Document the importer's purpose as configurable signal extraction rather than strict HTML reproduction, including at least two distinct use cases with different noise-versus-signal priorities.
    criterion Add baseline correction coverage for importer behaviors that were clearly too destructive relative to the formatter's current semantics, especially around inline text whitespace, comment preservation, and line-width-driven prose output.
    criterion Ensure line-width-aware import output follows actual HTML whitespace semantics, including treating ordinary source line breaks as collapsible whitespace rather than inventing paragraph boundaries.
    criterion Add focused tests that cover contrasting import intents, including text-forward imports, structure-forward imports, comment-sensitive imports, and width-constrained prose examples, so the tradeoffs are visible and reproducible.
    criterion Defer broader API-shape and full compatibility work to follow-on tasks once the baseline corrections are stable and intentional.
  review-items
    item(status="covered") The task is now explicitly framed around baseline corrections and consistency fixes, not full importer-feature design or full formatter-option parity.
    item(status="covered") The importer now has focused coverage and implementation for preserve-more inline text whitespace, comment preservation by default, and line-width-aware output that can emit reflowed dotted prose blocks.
    item(status="covered") The current line-width work now treats ordinary HTML source line breaks as collapsible whitespace instead of inventing paragraph boundaries during import.
    item(status="open") Decide whether the remaining `collapse-single-nested` heuristic belongs in this task as a final correction pass or should move into a follow-on task about structural extraction policy.
    item(status="open") Split full formatter-setting compatibility and the first formatter/importer code-sharing pass into `0012`, then move importer API-shape and profile design into later follow-on tasks so `0007` can close on a clear baseline-corrections milestone.
